# default.map — the shipped strictness profile: RISK CATEGORY → review roles added to the floor.
#
# Why this file exists. The tier used to decide BOTH the composition of the review set and its depth,
# through a hardcoded three-branch `case` in delivery-lib.sh. So the five risk categories the classifier
# already computes (bin/select-pipeline.sh: security/auth, data/schema, infra/deploy, api/contract, deps)
# collapsed into one tier word and were discarded into `reasons=` — a ready signal with no addressee.
# Composition now comes from the categories; the tier keeps only depth.
#
# This is the Spec Kit preset model: a profile OVERRIDES the mapping, it never adds capability. An
# organisation ships its own file and leaves the core untouched. Override the path with
# $TEAM_BOOTSTRAP_PROFILE.
#
# Format: `<category><space/tab><role> [role...]`; `#` comments and blank lines ignored.
#
# TWO RULES THIS FILE CANNOT BREAK, enforced in tests/roles-alive.test.sh:
#   * ADD-ONLY. Entries are unioned into the tier-derived base set. A profile can never shrink a set the
#     paths already earned, and the >=1 independent-reviewer anti-collapse floor is never sized away.
#   * NO DEAD ENTRIES. Every role named here must have an agents/<slug>.md (or it cannot be dispatched)
#     AND a required verdict field in references/schemas/role-output.schema.json (or it cannot be
#     confirmed at closure). Every category key must be one select-pipeline.sh actually emits.
#
# NOW MAPPED (milestone 020) — chaos-engineer, test-designer, legal-compliance-checker,
# ip-contracts-reviewer. The four used to be unmapped because none declared a required field in
# role-output.schema.json: assigning them bought a dispatch and no confirmation, which is the decoy the
# whole verdict pipeline exists to reject. AC-24 gave each one (resilience_verdict, test_design_verdict,
# release_recommendation promoted to required, ip_verdict) — a new required handoff field, so a MAJOR
# bump by references/versioning.md, and a decision rather than a detail. Their routing signals come from
# Д2 §1.2, which supplies what the tree lacked:
#
#   * chaos-engineer shares infra/deploy with devops-platform, exactly as Д2 §1.2 offers
#     ("devops-platform (в ревью-режиме) или chaos-engineer"). They ask different questions of the same
#     diff: is the pipeline sound, and what happens when it fails.
#   * test-designer takes `no-tests` — non-doc work changed and no test file came with it. Under this
#     repo's own TDD gate that never fires on a healthy batch, which is the point: it fires exactly when
#     something shipped unreached.
#   * legal-compliance-checker takes `licence` (the licence TEXT changed — a compliance event) and
#     ip-contracts-reviewer takes `deps` (a manifest changed — an IP event, where a new transitive
#     dependency can carry a copyleft obligation). Д2 §1.2 names "лицензии и зависимости" for the pair;
#     splitting them by which signal each actually answers keeps both bindings load-bearing instead of
#     doubling two roles onto both categories and paying the fan-out twice (OQ-3, ADR-0016).
#
# STILL NOT MAPPED, on purpose — api/contract. Its natural owner is integration-verifier, but the
# category itself forces the `full` tier, whose base set ALREADY contains that role, so the mapping could
# never change an outcome. `bin/eval-role.sh --liveness` reports such a binding DEAD, and a no-op entry
# inflates the appearance of routing without adding any.

security/auth   security-reviewer
data/schema     data-schema-reviewer
deps            security-reviewer overengineering-reviewer ip-contracts-reviewer
ui              accessibility-reviewer
infra/deploy    devops-platform chaos-engineer
perf            performance-reviewer
licence         legal-compliance-checker
no-tests        test-designer
